================================================================================
  1.  WHAT THIS CRATE IS
================================================================================

cipherbox-pqc-native is the native binding for cipherbox-pqc-core.
It provides:

  - a re-export of the entire core API, so that Rust applications
    can use cipherbox-pqc-native and get everything the core crate
    provides without adding a second dependency;

  - six CLI binaries that wrap the core primitives for shell use;

  - no cryptographic code of its own. Every operation is delegated
    to cipherbox-pqc-core.

See the top-level README for status, threat model, and what the
core crate does and does not protect against. Nothing in this
document replaces that discussion.

The crate is unaudited. The CLI tools are convenient; they are not
certified. Do not use them to protect anything that matters until
you have read section 4 of the top-level README.


================================================================================
  2.  INSTALLATION
================================================================================

Build the binaries:

  cargo build --release

The binaries are produced at:

  target/release/cipherbox-pqc-keygen
  target/release/cipherbox-pqc-file-encrypt
  target/release/cipherbox-pqc-file-decrypt
  target/release/cipherbox-pqc-wrap
  target/release/cipherbox-pqc-unwrap
  target/release/cipherbox-pqc-verify
  target/release/cipherbox-pqc-room-verifier

Install system-wide (optional):

  cargo install --path .

To use the library in another Rust project:

  [dependencies]
  cipherbox-pqc-native = "0.7"

  // The entire core API is re-exported:
  use cipherbox_pqc_native::{MlKemKeyPair, room, file};


================================================================================
  3.  SECRET ARGUMENTS
================================================================================

Commands that take a secret accept three forms:

  file:PATH     Read the secret from a file. The file is read once
                and its contents are used as the value. This is the
                recommended form for scripts.

  env:NAME      Read the secret from the named environment variable.
                This is the recommended form when the caller is
                another program that can set an environment variable
                without putting the secret on the command line.

  -             Read the secret from standard input. Useful for
                piping from a password manager:
                    keepassxc-cli show ... | cipherbox-pqc-file-decrypt ...
                Reads until EOF or the first newline, depending on
                the command (see each command below).

  Plain literal

                A bare string is also accepted. The tool prints a
                warning to stderr explaining that the secret is now
                visible in shell history and in /proc. This form
                exists for interactive use and for tests, not for
                scripts.

The warning text is:

  warning: secret supplied as a plain literal. It is visible in shell
  history and in the process list. Use file:PATH, env:NAME, or - instead.

The warning goes to stderr. It does not affect exit codes or stdout.


================================================================================
  4.  KEY GENERATION
================================================================================

Usage:

  cipherbox-pqc-keygen --out DIR [--hybrid] [--level 768|1024]
                                  [--passphrase SECRET]

Options:

  --out DIR           Required. The directory to write into.
                      Created if it does not exist.

  --hybrid            Generate an X25519 + ML-KEM hybrid key pair
                      instead of a plain ML-KEM key pair.

  --level 768|1024    ML-KEM parameter set. Default is 768.

  --passphrase SECRET Encrypt the secret half with this passphrase
                      before writing. Accepts the secret forms from
                      section 3. If omitted, the secret is written
                      unencrypted with mode 0600.

Plain ML-KEM output:

  DIR/ek.b64               Base64 of the encapsulation key.
                           1184 bytes decoded for level 768;
                           1568 bytes decoded for level 1024.
                           Safe to share.

  DIR/dk_seed.b64          Base64 of the 64-byte seed (d || z).
                           Secret. Mode 0600.
                           Written only if --passphrase is not
                           supplied.

  DIR/dk_seed.enc.b64      Base64 of the passphrase-encrypted seed.
                           Written only if --passphrase is supplied.

Hybrid output:

  DIR/ek.b64               Base64 of the hybrid public key.
                           1216 bytes decoded for level 768;
                           1600 bytes decoded for level 1024.
                           Safe to share.

  DIR/hk_secret.b64        Base64 of the 34-byte self-describing
                           secret. Secret. Mode 0600.

  DIR/hk_secret.enc.b64    Base64 of the passphrase-encrypted secret.
                           Written only if --passphrase is supplied.

The tool never overwrites an existing file. If any output file
already exists, the command fails before writing anything. To
regenerate, delete the directory or use a new one.

Example:

  cipherbox-pqc-keygen --out keys --level 1024 --passphrase env:KEYGEN_PASS

  ls -la keys/
  # ek.b64             mode 0644 (public)
  # dk_seed.enc.b64    mode 0600 (secret, encrypted)

To decrypt the seed later:

  cipherbox-pqc-file-decrypt file:keys/dk_seed.enc.b64 \
                             --passphrase env:KEYGEN_PASS \
                             ciphertext.cbox plaintext.bin


================================================================================
  5.  FILE ENCRYPTION AND DECRYPTION
================================================================================

Encrypt:

  cipherbox-pqc-file-encrypt <ek_b64> IN OUT [CHUNK_SIZE]

  <ek_b64>       Either a base64-encoded public key, or a secret
                 argument (section 3) whose contents are base64.
                 The form file:keys/ek.b64 is the common case.

  IN             Path to the plaintext file. Must exist and be
                 readable.

  OUT            Path to the ciphertext file. Created or truncated.

  CHUNK_SIZE     Optional. Bytes per chunk. Default 65536.
                 Minimum 64, maximum 1048576.

  The ML-KEM level and the suite (plain or hybrid) are inferred
  from the public key length. See section 10.

Decrypt:

  cipherbox-pqc-file-decrypt [SECRET] IN OUT [--passphrase SECRET]

  SECRET         The decapsulation secret. Either:
                   file:keys/dk_seed.b64        64-byte seed
                   file:keys/hk_secret.b64      34-byte hybrid secret
                 The tool detects which one by length.
                 See section 10.

  IN             Path to the ciphertext file.

  OUT            Path to the plaintext file. See "Atomicity" below.

  --passphrase   If the secret was stored encrypted, pass the
                 passphrase here. Accepts the forms from section 3.

Atomicity. Decryption writes to a private temporary file in the
same directory as OUT, and renames it to OUT only after the last
chunk authenticates. If the ciphertext is truncated, tampered, or
encrypted to a different key, the temporary file is removed and
OUT is never created (or, if it already existed, is left
unchanged).

This is the property that distinguishes cipherbox-pqc-file-decrypt
from a naive streaming decryptor: a failure leaves no partial
plaintext on disk. The core crate cannot guarantee this itself —
it produces the plaintext as it goes — so the CLI takes
responsibility for the file-system side.

The temporary file is created with mode 0600 and a name derived
from OUT plus a random suffix. It is removed on error and on
success after the rename.

Example:

  # Encrypt
  cipherbox-pqc-file-encrypt file:keys/ek.b64 large.bin large.cbox 262144

  # Decrypt
  cipherbox-pqc-file-decrypt file:keys/dk_seed.enc.b64 \
                             large.cbox large.recovered.bin \
                             --passphrase env:PASS

  # Verify (optional)
  cmp large.bin large.recovered.bin && echo "roundtrip OK"

Chunk size. Larger chunks are faster but give coarser truncation
detection. There is no correct answer for every workload. 64 KiB
to 1 MiB is the range most workloads want; the default (64 KiB)
is safe.


================================================================================
  6.  KEY WRAPPING AND UNWRAPPING
================================================================================

Wrap. Encapsulate to a public key, derive an AEAD key from the
shared secret, encrypt the payload, and emit a self-describing
JSON blob.

  cipherbox-pqc-wrap <ek_b64> -

  <ek_b64>       Base64-encoded public key, or a secret argument
                 whose contents are base64. Accepts plain ML-KEM
                 keys (1184 or 1568 bytes) and hybrid keys
                 (1216 or 1600 bytes).

  -              The payload to wrap is read from stdin. Reads
                 until EOF. The payload is not length-limited
                 beyond what the OS can buffer, but it is a room
                 key or similar, so it is expected to be short.

Output (stdout, single line):

  {"v":2,"ct":"...","iv":"...","enc_aes":"..."}

The three fields are:

  ct         base64 of the KEM ciphertext
  iv         base64 of the ChaCha20-Poly1305 nonce
  enc_aes    base64 of the encrypted payload plus AEAD tag

Unwrap:

  cipherbox-pqc-unwrap [SECRET] <v> <ct> <iv> <enc_aes>

  SECRET         The decapsulation secret. Same forms as
                 cipherbox-pqc-file-decrypt. Level and suite are
                 inferred (section 10).

  v              Version number. Read it from the JSON: {"v":2,...}.
                 Pass 1 to decrypt a legacy v1 blob.

  ct, iv, enc_aes
                 The three base64 fields from the JSON.

  Plaintext is written to stdout.

Example:

  # Sender: wrap a room key for a recipient.
  echo -n "room-key-abc-123" | cipherbox-pqc-wrap file:keys/bob_ek.b64 - > wrapped.json

  # Recipient: unwrap.
  V=$(jq -r .v wrapped.json)
  CT=$(jq -r .ct wrapped.json)
  IV=$(jq -r .iv wrapped.json)
  ENC=$(jq -r .enc_aes wrapped.json)

  cipherbox-pqc-unwrap file:keys/bob_dk_seed.b64 "$V" "$CT" "$IV" "$ENC"

Read the v field. Do not assume. Passing 2 for a legacy v1 blob
produces a "wrong key or tampered data" error by design.


================================================================================
  7.  SIGNATURE VERIFICATION
================================================================================

  cipherbox-pqc-verify <vk_b64> <msg_b64> <sig_b64>

  <vk_b64>       Base64 of the verifying key. The parameter set
                 is inferred from the decoded length:
                   1952 bytes -> ML-DSA-65
                   2592 bytes -> ML-DSA-87

  <msg_b64>      Base64 of the message. The message is signed
                 exactly as the bytes it decodes to; there is no
                 domain prefix added by this tool.

  <sig_b64>      Base64 of the signature. Length is checked
                 against the parameter set.

Behaviour:

  Prints "true" and exits 0 if the signature is valid.
  Prints "false" and exits 1 if the signature is invalid, the
  key or signature has the wrong length, or the base64 is
  malformed.

  There is no case in which the tool panics on malformed input.

Example:

  VK=$(base64 -w0 verifying_key.bin)
  MSG=$(echo -n "the message" | base64 -w0)
  SIG=$(base64 -w0 signature.bin)

  cipherbox-pqc-verify "$VK" "$MSG" "$SIG" && echo "valid" || echo "invalid"


================================================================================
  8.  ROOM VERIFIER
================================================================================

  cipherbox-pqc-room-verifier <auth_token_b64>

  <auth_token_b64>
                 Base64 of the 32-byte auth token from a
                 secret-derived room. Produced by
                 RoomSecret::auth_token() in the core crate.

Output (stdout): the 32-byte verifier, base64-encoded.

The verifier is what a server should store. The flow:

  1. Client derives a room secret from shared sources (a
     passphrase, a photo hash, whatever the room agrees on).

  2. Client computes auth_token = H("auth", root) and tells the
     server:
       - room_id   (safe to publish)
       - verifier  (on room creation)

  3. Server stores { room_id -> verifier } and nothing else. No
     secret, no member list, no keys.

  4. On every subsequent request, client sends auth_token. Server
     computes room_auth_verifier(auth_token) and compares against
     the stored verifier. If they match, the client is a member
     of the room.

The server cannot derive the secret from either the token or the
verifier. The token is a bearer credential: whoever has it can
act as a member. Do not log it.

Rust servers can skip the CLI and call the core function directly:

  use cipherbox_pqc_native::room;

  let verifier = room::auth_verifier(&auth_token);
  // or, if you have the room secret:
  let verifier = room::auth_verifier(&secret.auth_token());

The CLI exists for servers written in other languages, or for
shell scripts that populate a database.


================================================================================
  9.  HYBRID KEYS
================================================================================

A hybrid key pair combines X25519 and ML-KEM. It is secure if
either holds. Generation:

  cipherbox-pqc-keygen --out keys --hybrid

Output:

  keys/ek.b64             1216 or 1600 bytes, base64. Share this.
  keys/hk_secret.b64      34 bytes, base64. Secret. Encrypt with
                          --passphrase before storing.

Every tool that accepts a public key accepts a hybrid public key:

  cipherbox-pqc-file-encrypt file:keys/ek.b64 in.bin out.cbox
  cipherbox-pqc-wrap         file:keys/ek.b64 -

Every tool that accepts a secret accepts a hybrid secret:

  cipherbox-pqc-file-decrypt file:keys/hk_secret.b64 in.cbox out.bin
  cipherbox-pqc-unwrap       file:keys/hk_secret.b64 2 ...

The tool detects a hybrid secret by its length (34 bytes decoded)
versus a plain ML-KEM seed (64 bytes decoded). You do not need to
tell it which is which.

A file encrypted to a hybrid key cannot be decrypted with a plain
ML-KEM key of the same level, and vice versa. The decryptor
enforces this at the file header check and exits with a specific
error.


================================================================================
 10.  LEVEL INFERENCE
================================================================================

You never tell the CLI tools which ML-KEM level you are using.
They infer it from the data:

  Encrypting and wrapping.
      From the decoded public key length:
        1184  -> ML-KEM-768
        1568  -> ML-KEM-1024
        1216  -> X25519 + ML-KEM-768
        1600  -> X25519 + ML-KEM-1024
      Any other length is rejected with an error.

  Decrypting a file.
      From the file header (see top-level README section 8).
      A single seed can decrypt files at either level; the file
      itself records which was used.

  Unwrapping a wrapped key.
      From the KEM ciphertext size:
        1088  -> ML-KEM-768
        1568  -> ML-KEM-1024
        1120  -> X25519 + ML-KEM-768
        1600  -> X25519 + ML-KEM-1024

  Verifying a signature.
      From the verifying-key length:
        1952  -> ML-DSA-65
        2592  -> ML-DSA-87

The only place this is not automatic is keygen, where --level is
required to choose which key pair to generate.


================================================================================
 11.  SAFETY PROPERTIES OF THE CLI TOOLS
================================================================================

These are properties of the CLI binaries, not of the core library.

Keygen never overwrites.
      If any output file exists, the command fails before writing
      anything. Partial key generation is not possible.

Keygen writes secrets with mode 0600.
      The secret file is readable only by the owner. The public
      key file is mode 0644. This is enforced on Unix; on Windows
      the standard file ACL is used.

File-decrypt is atomic.
      Decryption writes to a private temporary file and renames
      it to the output path only after the last chunk
      authenticates. A failed or truncated decryption leaves no
      output file. If the output path already existed, its
      previous contents are untouched.

File-decrypt uses a private temporary.
      The temporary file is created with mode 0600 and a name
      that cannot be predicted by another process. It is removed
      on both success (after rename) and failure.

Secrets are not echoed.
      The warning printed for plain-literal secrets goes to
      stderr and does not include the secret itself. The tools
      do not print secrets to stdout unless the command's purpose
      is to produce them (e.g. unwrap writes plaintext to
      stdout).

Verify never panics.
      cipherbox-pqc-verify on malformed base64, wrong-length key,
      wrong-length signature, or an invalid signature prints
      "false" and exits 1. There is no input that causes a panic
      in that binary.

Exit codes.
      0 on success.
      1 on any failure, including a signature that does not
      verify (which is a valid outcome of a verification, not a
      tool error).


================================================================================
 12.  USING THE LIBRARY INSTEAD OF THE CLI
================================================================================

Every CLI tool is a thin wrapper. If you are writing a Rust
program, call the library directly.

  use cipherbox_pqc_native::{
      MlKemKeyPair, HybridKeyPair,
      file, room,
      encapsulate_to, wrap_key, unwrap_key,
  };

  // Key generation.
  let kp = MlKemKeyPair::new()?;
  let (seed, ek) = kp.export_keys();

  // File encryption.
  let ct = file::encrypt_file(plaintext, &ek, 65536)?;
  let pt = file::decrypt_file(&ct, kp.decapsulation_key())?;

  // Key wrapping.
  let blob = wrap_key(&ek, b"room-key")?;
  let room_key = unwrap_key(&kp, &blob)?;

  // Room verifier.
  let verifier = room::auth_verifier(&auth_token);

Everything the CLI does is one library call away. The CLI adds:
secret argument parsing, base64 encoding and decoding, atomic
file writes, and consistent exit codes. If you have written a
Rust program, you probably want to do those things yourself.

The full API surface of the core crate is re-exported. See the
core crate's README and USER_GUIDE.md for the complete reference.


================================================================================
 13.  WHERE TO GO NEXT
================================================================================

Read the top-level README section 4 before using any of these
tools to protect anything that matters. It describes the trust
barriers (unaudited, not ACVP-validated) and the threat-model
boundaries (no constant-time analysis on the compiled binary).

Read the core crate's USER_GUIDE.md for the library API. Every
section of that guide has a direct counterpart in this crate's
re-export surface.

If you need an audited tool for the same operations, the
RustCrypto ml-kem crate and the ml-dsa crate both provide
wasm-compatible and native-compatible APIs, and both have been
independently assessed.


